Z||000017||Sillhouette.com||06-26-2000||05:04 PM||||The best system is not necessarly the nicest.  The picture with a red fiero sent in by Monkey man is them most efficient way to get rid of heat from the radiator.  It is just using a 1990 Trans AM grille cut your hood and that is it.  The other designs are very nice and look aerodynamically pleasing but they only work if the car is moving forward.  If the car is static those vents do not do anything.  The problem for cooling in our cars is not while moving but when stuck in a traffic jam.  Another way to lower the temperature is to get a bigger rad and punch some more holes into your thermostat, or rewrite the ECM chip to start the fan at a lower temperature from the standard 160. <P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||06-26-2000||07:47 PM||||Actually Still... I'm guessing the hood vents will help somewhat even at idle, if the fan was running. Turning the fan on at a lower temp is a good idea. Rodney Dickman has low temp switches, I think. I don't recommend drilling or punching holes in the thermostat. Put in a $5 Slant 180 degree stat instead (part #13818).||||reg||1
Z||000019||hugh||06-26-2000||08:21 PM||||Batboy,The base of the windshield is a high pressure area,not the best place to exhaust air.I have the front fender vents from a (I believe)late 80's camaro in the hood of my Grand National.They look good and they are functional.If you don't have some means of relieving pressure under the hood.Even at speeds of 50,60,70 MPH the air through your radiator is only moving at about 20 MPH or less.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Haze_Performance||06-26-2000||09:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hood.jpg">   <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Whats up with that hood?? Do you have a full car picture??<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000021||TheFiero64||06-27-2000||11:35 AM||||The Question I have about putting a scoop on the hood is? Would this make the rear compartment run hotter? The air from the radiator is what blows under the car up into the engine area and out the side vents(<I>bbq grill vents on an 84</I>). Removing this are by cutting the hood will increase the engine temp drastically. right?||||reg||5
Z||000022||batboy||06-27-2000||02:56 PM||||There is air flowing under the car at all times, much of it did not go through the radiator, but some of it did. If say, 1/2 the hot radiator air is exiting out a hood vent and going over the car instead, then the air flowing under the car is cooler by several degrees. This cooler air is sucked up into the engine compartment and then the engine heated air exits through the deck louvers. Other advantages to the front hood scoops, vents, and/or louvers is that they reduce the air pooling in the radiator area of the nose that slows down air movement through the radiator fins and causes high speed lift of the front end. You can't lose. Just be sure you don't cut any hood supports and only cut the hood within the sealed radiator area. You don't want water pouring into the spare tire well all the time.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||lowCG||06-27-2000||08:52 PM||||I cut out the area that Batboy just mentioned,easy enough,as it's contained by structure,which served as a nice guide for my jig-saw.I started by drilling a series of small holes to guide me as I sawed away from the top.Added a three mil radius to the sharp edge,a grill,and presto.<BR>Works great,less filling.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||batboy||06-27-2000||09:56 PM||||I'd better put my hood louvers in now that I've went and ran off at the mouth...LOL. I have a pair of Trans Am louvers like those on the orange Fiero pic that Monkey posted. Time to fire up the saber saw and do it. Already have a template made, better get busy.||||reg||1
Z||000025||theogre||06-28-2000||05:58 PM||||I've covered alot of this in the article in the cave, so I won't go over it all again here. I'm still looking at front end cooling so there will be an addon to the cave article in future. For now,<P>The hood vent will help even at idle. Hot air rises even w/o the fan. The problem at idle is simply a lack of movement thru the radiator. Fiero radiator is already more than big enough. Only running the fan fixes the idle problem. You have the same problem on nearly every car if the fan fails. <P>I don't believe ducting thru the tubes as descibed & drawn above will help much at all. You'd have to use some rather large duct to do any good. You'd have to pretty much eliminate the storage area. The air does pile up there, but the fan stirs it and blows it out the bottom quite well.<P>The best solution still is the low temp fan switch for most people, even with hood vents. I've been looking for a good, reliable, way to add a low temp switch to the DIS motors as well. (The DIS ECM controls the fan. these don't use a fan switch.) Since the ECM doesn't know the fan is on or off adding a switch won't screw up the ECM programming. (If you turn on the AC the fan comes on, but an ECM scanner will still say the fans are off.) I'm thinking a radiator mounted switch is probly easiest to setup for DIS. Since the fan relay is right there the wiring is easy. Just an issue of finding a good switch, and time to do it. (Yes, you could do a switch in the DIS motor, but there's more wire and you have to open a port in the head for the switch. The radiator switches can be installed w/o opening the cooling system at all. Many are even adjustable.)<BR>||||reg||1
